---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CircleCropImage from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CircleCropImage.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="circle-crop-image">
  <CircleCropImage client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to crop a photo into a circle</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop a photo onto the box above, or several to make avatars for a whole team.</li>
      <li>Choose the shape: a full circle, a rounded square (with an adjustable corner radius) or a plain square.</li>
      <li>Set the output size. The image scales to cover the frame, so the edges are never empty.</li>
      <li>Drag the preview to reposition it, or use the zoom and position sliders for precise keyboard control.</li>
      <li>Optionally add a border ring and a solid background, then export as PNG for real transparency.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why the output is a PNG</h2>
    <p>
      A circle inside a square file needs transparent corners, not white ones. That needs an alpha
      channel, which PNG and WebP have and JPEG does not. A circular crop exported as JPEG gets solid
      black corners, or the background colour you set. If a site accepts only JPEG uploads, turn on the
      solid background and match it to the page colour behind the avatar.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you control the site, consider WebP: it supports transparency and gives files about a quarter
      the size of the same PNG.
    </p>

    <h2>Profile picture sizes across platforms</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Displayed at</th><th>Upload</th><th>Shape</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Slack</td><td>up to 512 px</td><td>512 × 512</td><td>Rounded square</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Discord</td><td>128 px</td><td>512 × 512</td><td>Circle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>LinkedIn</td><td>400 px</td><td>400 × 400 or larger</td><td>Circle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GitHub</td><td>460 px</td><td>500 × 500</td><td>Rounded square</td></tr>
          <tr><td>X (Twitter)</td><td>400 px</td><td>400 × 400</td><td>Circle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Instagram</td><td>320 px</td><td>320 × 320 or larger</td><td>Circle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Zoom / Teams</td><td>256 px</td><td>512 × 512</td><td>Circle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gravatar</td><td>up to 2048 px</td><td>512 × 512</td><td>Square (cropped by site)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A square 512 × 512 export covers almost all cases. A much larger upload is only downscaled by the
      platform, and an upload smaller than the display size is the usual cause of a soft, blurry avatar.
    </p>

    <h2>Framing a headshot well</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Fill the frame. The head and shoulders should fill most of the circle. A full-body shot cropped to 128 px is an unrecognisable smudge.</li>
      <li>Put the eyes on the upper third. Leave a little space above the head, not a lot; most people crop too loose.</li>
      <li>Watch the corners. A circular mask removes about 21% of a square image. Keep important content inside the inscribed circle.</li>
      <li>Check it small. Zoom your browser out until the preview is about 40 px across. If you cannot recognise the person, crop tighter.</li>
      <li>Contrast the background. Avatars show on both light and dark UI. A subject that blends into a mid-grey background disappears in one of them; a border ring fixes this.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Rounded squares and the corner-radius number</h2>
    <p>
      The radius slider is a percentage of the output size, so it stays proportional at any export
      resolution. 50% gives a full circle. About 22–25% matches the “squircle” look of iOS and macOS
      app icons, and 8–12% gives the gentle rounding of most web card components.
    </p>

    <h2>Batch avatars for a team</h2>
    <p>
      Add all headshots at once and set the options on the first image. The tool renders the whole
      batch with the same shape, size, border and zoom. Each photo is scaled to cover its own frame, so
      portrait and landscape sources all come out square. Download them together as a ZIP. If the
      framing of the source photos varies a lot, <a href="/crop-image">pre-crop</a> the odd ones so
      the faces have a consistent scale.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
